  "native_text": "was not the result of administrative error; it was the calculated execution of an industrial-scale \nscheme designed to shift risk, bypass borrowing costs, and exploit the T+6 Continuous Net \nSettlement loophole.1 Bleich’s concurrent use of Cayman Islands master-feeder structures and \nDelaware liability ring-fencing further demonstrates a systemic reliance on architectural \nopacity to avoid regulatory scrutiny and aggregate institutional capital without transparency.1 \nWhen Bleich transitioned into domestic civil litigation against Arnulf Damerau, he deployed the \nexact same operational philosophy.1 In collaboration with Robert A. Giacovas—an attorney with \na documented history of SLAPP sanctions, fabricated quotes, and administrative \ndelinquency—Bleich weaponized the New York State judicial system.1 By misrepresenting \nproperty rights against recorded Condominium Declarations, circumventing Hague Convention \nservice protocols, and concealing illegal municipal violations to secure preliminary injunctions, \nBleich manufactured a legal stalemate designed purely for economic extortion.1 \nUltimately, Bleich approaches the legal system exactly as he approached fragmented equity \nmarkets: as an inefficient plumbing architecture to be exploited through bad faith, procedural \narbitrage, and asymmetric leverage. For prospective counterparties, institutional allocators \nbound by ESG mandates, and judicial tribunals, entities directed by Michael Bleich present \nunmitigable reputational and operational risks requiring extreme, continuous scrutiny.1 The data \nstrongly and repeatedly suggests that in scenarios where proprietary profitability and \nregulatory compliance intersect, Bleich's enterprises systematically prioritize the former, \nregardless of the systemic damage or legal consequences.1 \nWorks cited \n1.​ Drafting Judiciary Law § 487 Complaint.pdf \n2.​ Bleich v Board of Mgrs. of 13 Harrison St. Condominium - Justia Law, accessed \nFebruary 23, 2026, \nhttps://law.justia.com/cases/new-york/other-courts/2025/2025-ny-slip-op-30182-\nu.html \n3.​ Bleich v Board of Mgrs. of 13 Harrison St. Condominium - Justia Law, accessed \nFebruary 23, 2026, \nhttps://law.justia.com/cases/new-york/other-courts/2025/2025-ny-slip-op-32009\n-u.html \n",
